#!/usr/bin/env bash
# post-clear-reground.sh — SessionStart hook, matcher "clear|resume".
# Fires after a /clear or a --continue/--resume: the conversation (and any
# loaded skill) is gone, CLAUDE.md and the memory INDEX reload, but the BODIES
# of memory files do not. This stdout is injected into the fresh context so the
# session re-grounds its fleet + subagent operating discipline before acting.
# Sibling of post-compact-reground.sh (matcher "compact").
# Must be fast and fail-open: static text only, no network calls, no state reads.
# Rendered by the engsys installer; static content, no project fills.
cat <<'EOF'
Context was just cleared or resumed. CLAUDE.md and the memory INDEX reloaded, but your prior conversation, any loaded skill, and the memory-file BODIES did NOT. Re-ground before acting:

1. ROLE SKILL — if this session runs a long-lived role skill (merge-monster, maintenance-monster, or similar): re-invoke it, or re-read its SKILL.md + config (.claude/*.yml, or the fleet-config-dir copy whose path heads state.md) + <state_dir>/state.md, then reconcile against live GitHub per its Session-startup steps. A /clear drops the skill; a launch script that primes the role via a first-turn slash command does NOT re-fire it after a clear.

2. SUBAGENT ORCHESTRATION — the discipline most easily lost on a clear:
   - Dispatch that reports back vs. goes idle: an async background agent (no name) returns its result to you automatically via a task-notification; a NAMED teammate agent goes idle holding its result and you must message it to pull it. Prefer async dispatch when you just want an answer back.
   - Never fire-and-forget: after spawning a background/async agent, wait for its completion notification (or track it) before you report or move on — and never narrate, assume, or predict a pending agent's result.
   - Long runners (> a few minutes): use the subagent-liveness skill (register at spawn, watch for stale/overdue) so a quiet death never leaves you idle on a corpse.
   - Verify live state: read PR / issue / CI / merge state from GitHub, not from memory — a merge can leave a PR OPEN; confirm it actually merged before calling it done.
   - If a merge orchestrator owns the merge baton: do NOT merge yourself. Finish the PR (local review clean + pre-push gate green + threads resolved), leave it a draft, and hand off per your merge-orchestration protocol.

3. Treat any precise value you "remember" (PR#, issue#, SHA, counter, deadline) as UNVERIFIED until re-checked from files or GitHub.

Full detail: CLAUDE.md § "Subagent orchestration" and § "Context compaction".
EOF
